          {
            "caution_id": "evidence-gap:99fe02c787e6e20c9ee3271d",
            "description": "Chromatin accessibility and clonotype capabilities: These are prospective extensions in the paper, not implemented totalVI claims.",
            "evidence": [
              {
                "content_hash": "d4570a0b97c163978a9f754c53ba1dd6f48e7331841109a5c5aac800bcd32bdd",
                "end_offset": 35609,
                "evidence_span_id": "evidence-span:e9fd478080f22735c03580a8",
                "exact_text": "Through a single pipeline that jointly analyzes paired RNA and protein measurements, totalVI simplifies data analysis and interpretation that would otherwise be conducted in separate pipelines whose disparate results must be reconciled post hoc. totalVI is available through the scvi-tools software package, which connects it with the popular Scanpy [56] and Seurat [34] pipelines, and enables analysis on free cloud computing environments like Google Colab. The flexibility and scalability of totalVI make it easily applicable to future datasets with larger protein panels, and enable extensions that incorporate additional paired measurements. For example, we expect totalVI to naturally handle intracellular proteins measured with barcoded antibodies. Further additions of modalities like chromatin accessibility [57] or clonotype features [58] can also be implemented within the totalVI codebase with consideration of the modality-specific likelihood. By combining multiple views of cellular processes, totalVI can reveal a more complete picture that redefines cell states and elucidates mechanistic relationships between molecular components of the cell.",
                "locator": {
                  "kind": "section",
                  "value": "source-artifact:884e738e91cfb0cab46306828a98280f/xml:/article/body/sec[3]/p[4];text-sha256:3766e1d43421d5144d2a92c51830bb6ffc8e9e12f94c524c377faacb41a9aa1e:34450:35609"
